# Threads, Channels & Shared State

## Prefer message passing

```rust
use std::sync::mpsc;
use std::thread;

let (tx, rx) = mpsc::channel();
thread::spawn(move || {
    for n in 1..=3 { tx.send(n).expect("receiver alive"); }
});
for received in rx {                       // blocks until senders drop
    println!("got {received}");
}
```

*Share memory by communicating.* Ownership moves through the channel, so races
cannot happen by construction.

## Shared state needs Arc + Mutex

```rust
use std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};

let counter = Arc::new(Mutex::new(0u64));
let handles: Vec<_> = (0..4).map(|_| {
    let counter = Arc::clone(&counter);
    thread::spawn(move || {
        *counter.lock().expect("poison") += 1;
    })
}).collect();
for h in handles { h.join().expect("worker panicked"); }
println!("{}", counter.lock().expect("poison")); // 4
```

- `Send`: type may move to another thread.
- `Sync`: `&T` may be shared across threads.
- `Mutex<T>` turns `T` into Sync; `Arc` shares ownership atomically.

Hold the lock guard the minimum time; never `.lock()` twice on one thread.

examples.code:
use std::sync::mpsc;
use std::thread;

fn main() {
    let (tx, rx) = mpsc::channel();
    let workers: Vec<_> = (0..3)
        .map(|id| {
            let tx = tx.clone();
            thread::spawn(move || {
                tx.send(format!("work from {id}")).expect("send");
            })
        })
        .collect();
    drop(tx); // so rx iteration ends
    for msg in rx {
        println!("{msg}");
    }
    for w in workers {
        w.join().expect("join");
    }
}

